  "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-6u+7 \\\\ 4u+3v-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6u+v \\\\ 4u+3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7u-2v \\\\ 5u+v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7u-2v \\\\ 5u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn fact, the transformation ${T}_{2}$ is a linear transformation.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not affine since it contains the cross term $uv.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only.\"",
  "universe": [
    {
      "label": "T1",
      "text": "$T_1:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\mapsto\\begin{bmatrix}v-6u+7\\\\4u+3v-5\\end{bmatrix}$",
      "true": true,
      "proof": "A=[[-6,1],[4,3]], b=[7,-5]."
    },
    {
      "label": "T2",
      "text": "$T_2:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\mapsto\\begin{bmatrix}-7u-2v\\\\5u+v\\end{bmatrix}$",
      "true": true,
      "proof": "A=[[-7,-2],[5,1]], b=[0,0]."
    },
    {
      "label": "T3",
      "text": "$T_3:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\mapsto\\begin{bmatrix}2u+v\\\\uv+1\\end{bmatrix}$",
      "true": false,
      "proof": "Cross term uv cannot be represented by a constant matrix plus constant translation."
    }
  ],
